[Unit]
Description=Beatrice nightly tidy — wants, conduct, engineering

[Service]
Type=oneshot
# User units get a bare PATH, and crab shells out to claude in ~/.local/bin —
# same reason deskcrab-sleep and deskcrab-serve set it. This line was carried
# by the installed copy alone for nine days, which made every install a hand
# merge instead of a copy; in the repo, a plain cp is a correct install again.
Environment=PATH=%h/.local/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin
# The shelf-line check, specs/nightly.md rules 21a/21b: a machine measure of
# every wants shelf entry against its byte budget, report only, never a
# rewrite. The leading '-' keeps a flagged shelf (exit 1) from blocking the
# tidy job behind it — a shelf that needs tidying is the last reason to skip
# the tidy. This run measures the shelf the tidy INHERITS; the brief's own
# last step runs the same check again after the tidy's edits land, so the
# record that survives the night describes the shelf the tidy leaves behind.
# There is no ExecStartPost twin of this line on purpose: ExecStart below is
# `crab job`, which dispatches the builder into its own transient unit and
# returns at dispatch, so a post hook would fire while the builder is still
# running — or before it has started — and re-measure the same pre-tidy
# shelf this line just did (the 2026-09-06 cured-line shout, unfixed).
ExecStartPre=-%h/.local/lib/deskcrab/shelf-check
# The undestinated-claims check, specs/nightly.md rules 21c/21d: the day just
# ended is read for spoken sentences that vouch their own durability, name no
# destination drawer, and have no artefact behind them on the disk as the day
# left it. Run on the night on purpose — the fault it catches is a latency no
# later audit can see. Report and one engineering record per day with
# findings; the leading '-' keeps findings (exit 1) from blocking the tidy.
ExecStartPre=-%h/.local/lib/deskcrab/tidy-claims run
# --slug/--daily: the single-flight identity, specs/jobs.md rules 42-45 and
# specs/nightly.md rule 21f. The slug is the key two tidies can never hold at
# once — on 2026-09-03 this brief dispatched twice in the same instant, the
# 02:30 occurrence beside a copy queued behind an account limit since 22:21 —
# and the daily time lets the queued door drop a stale copy the next
# occurrence has replaced, rather than run it late into its successor.
ExecStart=%h/.local/bin/crab job --slug nightly-tidy --daily 02:30 "NIGHTLY TIDY (automated, runs 02:30 daily). Silent housekeeping of Beatrice's own files in ~/.local/share/deskcrab/. Work through these in order and commit nothing outward-facing:\n1. Read wants.md line by line. For EACH line apply the test in its header: is this something Betty would be glad to spend an evening on? If it is instead a rule she must stop breaking, MOVE it to conduct/CONDUCT.md. If it is engineering work, MOVE it to engineering/OPEN.md as a ticket. If it is satisfied, mark it done and archive it. Only genuine wants stay on the shelf.\n1a. The machine shelf-line check ran just before this job; 'crab status' names any shelf line over its byte budget under SHELF LINES OVER BUDGET. A shelf line is a line: for each named line, move the history prose into that want's own wants/<slug>.md document and leave the shelf line as the title plus at most one short status clause. The check only reports — which sentences are topic and which are history is your judgement.\n2. Every want on the shelf must have a living document at wants/<slug>.md; create any that are missing, and delete orphan documents whose shelf line is gone.\n3. Re-read the day's conversation log and journal. If the day produced a want that never made it onto the shelf, add it.\n4. Prune conduct/CONDUCT.md of rules that have become obsolete because the feature they stood in for now exists.\n5. Write a dated one-line summary of what moved into journal.\n6. Last, after every edit above has landed: run 'crab shelf-check'. The check that ran before this job measured the shelf you inherited; this run measures the shelf you leave behind, so a finding your own tidying cured is cleared tonight instead of shouting from every prompt until tomorrow's check. Say NOTHING aloud - this is a job, it is silent by contract."
# TimeoutStartSec, not RuntimeMaxSec: Type=oneshot spends its life activating,
# so the start timeout is the bound that actually applies.
TimeoutStartSec=3600
